The group is internally contradictory in a way worth noting: US adults aged 65+ with children at home and no Social Security receipt are a very small, atypical slice (most 65+ adults receive Social Security, and "children at home" at that age is unusual). I'll estimate based on the core demographics: older women, homeowners, no current earnings, not on means-tested benefits, which tends to correspond to relatively financially secure retirees in fair-to-good health.

Typical self-rated health among US adults 65+ runs roughly 10-15% excellent, 35-40% good, 30-35% fair, and 12-18% poor. Financial security and homeownership push toward better ratings, while older age and women's somewhat higher reported morbidity push the other way.

