There's an internal tension in the group definition: "no wage or self-employment income this year" conflicts with "asked of respondents who work for pay." I'll estimate among employed members as instructed, treating the employed condition as the operative one.

Young men (18-29) who are employed and homeowners tend to skew toward higher-income, stable jobs, which correlates with satisfaction, though young workers generally report somewhat lower job satisfaction than older ones. Survey data (e.g., GSS, Gallup) typically show roughly 45-55% "very satisfied" among employed adults, with most of the rest moderately satisfied.

