The described group is internally inconsistent in a way that matters: US adults aged 65+ are overwhelmingly receiving Social Security (the vast majority of this age group), and "with children at home" plus "aged 65+" is unusual. Also, having no income of any kind this year while not receiving Social Security is rare among 65+ adults. This is a small, atypical slice, likely drawn from people relying on accumulated savings or assets, which tends to produce a wider spread of savings levels than the general population, with homeownership implying some home equity but not necessarily liquid savings.

Among those with no earned income and no Social Security or means-tested benefits, savings are likely concentrated in a middle-to-upper range (retirement account drawdowns, investments), though many in such a position may have modest liquid cash. I estimate a distribution weighted toward the $1,000+ categories but with meaningful mass at lower levels.

